</style>
<h2>Asobi Review 1.0 (build 1611) <span class="when">Oct 15, 2026</span></h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Kitsu against the disk.</b> Kitsu&#8230; in Shots on Disk sets the sequence&#8217;s shots in Kitsu beside its folders and fixes what you tick: a shot renamed on disk renamed in Kitsu, a shot merged into another marked omitted, a shot back in the cut marked in the cut again, a folder with plates and no Kitsu shot made in Kitsu. Try shows what fixing would do without doing it. Nothing is deleted in Kitsu.</li>
<li><b>Move a shot to another sequence.</b> The rename sheet&#8217;s Sequence field moves a shot and its plates to another sequence of the job, with a number there (<code>SST_RAI_040</code> becomes <code>SST_BOU_045</code>). Written down and undoable like a rename.</li>
<li><b>A shot cut in two.</b> In Pull Plates the new half reads &#8220;New, split from 040&#8221;; Swap Names gives it 040 instead, when the work on 040 belongs to that half.</li>
<li><b>A folder two tools wrote into</b> gets a Server Script: Asobi writes a script for the server that sets aside the frames that don&#8217;t belong, by their exact names, with a dry run and an undo. It never moves them itself over the Mac&#8217;s view of the server.</li>
<li><b>A shot the cut brings back</b> keeps its number, and Kitsu&#8230; marks it in the cut again.</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<div class="earlier">Earlier updates</div>
